            <ArticleTitle>Risk factors associated with failure of extubation in very-low-birth-weight newborns.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>300-304</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1080/14767058.2017.1285884</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To identify risk factors associated with the failure of extubation of mechanically ventilated very-low-birth-weight newborns.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Prospective observational study. Assessment of the occurrence of extubation failure in relation to demographic and ventilation parameters, the SpO2/FiO2 ratio, the spontaneous breathing test (SBT) and values of the Silverman-Andersen score (SAS). Extubation failure was defined as the need for reintubation for any reason within 72 h after extubation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Extubation failed in 14/50 (28%) patients. Tidal volume applied at the moment of extubation (p = 0.030), the values of the SpO2/FiO2 ratio (p = 0.006), SBT (p = 0.034) and SAS measured for 60 min after extubation and later (p = 0.010, p = 0.000001, p∼0.000, respectively) showed a significant association with reintubation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Measured TV, SpO2/FiO2 ratio, SBT at the moment of extubation and values of SAS starting 1 h after extubation might be valuable parameters in identifying those VLBW newborns in the risk to fail extubation.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
